~~~

### Hosting model: generic host, Kestrel and IIS
- **Generic host** ({{WebApplication.CreateBuilder}}) owns configuration, logging, DI, hosted services and lifetime (start, graceful stop). A web app is a host with a Kestrel server and an HTTP pipeline added; a worker service is the same host without HTTP.
- **Kestrel** is the cross-platform, high-performance server inside every ASP.NET Core app. In containers it faces traffic directly behind a load balancer or ingress.
- **IIS in-process** runs the app inside the IIS worker process (w3wp) via the ASP.NET Core Module: fastest option on Windows. **Out-of-process** runs Kestrel as a separate process with IIS as a reverse proxy: needed for some isolation setups, slower.
- Behind any proxy, configure **forwarded headers** so the app sees the real scheme and client IP, and only trust known proxies.

~~~csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);       // generic host: config, logging, DI
builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(k =>
{
    k.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 10 * 1024 * 1024;       // 10 MB default for this API
    k.AddServerHeader = false;
});
builder.Services.Configure<ForwardedHeadersOptions>(o =>
{
    o.ForwardedHeaders = ForwardedHeaders.XForwardedFor | ForwardedHeaders.XForwardedProto;
    o.KnownNetworks.Add(new Microsoft.AspNetCore.HttpOverrides.IPNetwork(IPAddress.Parse("10.0.0.0"), 8));
});
var app = builder.Build();
app.UseForwardedHeaders();                                // first, before anything reads scheme or IP
~~~

ASP.NET Core ships a constructor-injection container ({{IServiceCollection}} → {{IServiceProvider}}). Lifetimes: **Transient** (per resolution), **Scoped** (per request), **Singleton** (per app).

Rules: a service may depend only on equal or longer lifetimes; singletons must be thread-safe; never resolve scoped services from the root provider. Use {{IServiceScopeFactory}} in background services. Keyed services (.NET 8) allow multiple implementations by key. For features the built-in container lacks (decorators, assembly scanning) use Scrutor or Autofac.

~~~csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>();
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IClock, SystemClock>();
builder.Services.AddKeyedSingleton<IPaymentGateway, StripeGateway>("stripe");
~~~

Centralise: use {{UseExceptionHandler}} / {{IExceptionHandler}} (.NET 8) to log once and map exceptions to **RFC 9457 ProblemDetails** ({{application/problem+json}}) with a stable {{type}}, {{title}}, {{status}}, and a {{traceId}} extension. Never leak stack traces or internal messages in production.

Map domain failures to status codes deliberately (400 validation, 401/403 auth, 404, 409 conflict, 422, 429, 5xx). Expected failures are results, not exceptions. Add {{AddProblemDetails()}} so framework errors (404, 415) use the same shape.

~~~csharp
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();
app.UseExceptionHandler();
~~~

