questions.a:
- **Config from environment** (no baked secrets); one image for all environments.
- **Stateless processes**: sessions and caches external (Redis); local disk is ephemeral.
- **Logs to stdout** in structured form; the platform collects them.
- **Health endpoints** for probes; graceful shutdown on SIGTERM (finish in-flight requests within {{terminationGracePeriodSeconds}}).
- **Small images**: multi-stage Dockerfile, chiseled/alpine base, non-root user, {{DOTNET_}} env settings; set memory-aware GC ({{DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit}}) and CPU limits (the runtime reads cgroup limits).
- **Port binding** via {{ASPNETCORE_URLS}}; no HTTPS inside the cluster if the mesh/ingress handles TLS.
- **Dependencies versioned and scanned**; SBOM and vulnerability scan in CI.

### .NET Aspire: local orchestration and service defaults
.NET Aspire is an opinionated stack for building distributed .NET apps:
- **AppHost project**: describes your services and their dependencies (PostgreSQL, Redis, RabbitMQ, other services) in C#, starts them all locally with one command, and injects connection strings and service URLs.
- **Service defaults**: a shared project that wires OpenTelemetry, health checks, service discovery and HTTP resilience into every service the same way.
- **Dashboard**: logs, traces and metrics across all services locally, without installing an observability stack.
- **Deployment**: generates a manifest that tools turn into Azure Container Apps or Kubernetes resources; many teams use Aspire for local development and keep their own IaC for production.

~~~csharp
// AppHost/Program.cs
var builder = DistributedApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

var postgres = builder.AddPostgres("pg").AddDatabase("payments");
var redis    = builder.AddRedis("cache");
var bus      = builder.AddRabbitMQ("bus");

var api = builder.AddProject<Projects.Payments_Api>("payments-api")
    .WithReference(postgres).WithReference(redis).WithReference(bus);

builder.AddProject<Projects.Payments_Worker>("payments-worker").WithReference(postgres).WithReference(bus);
builder.AddNpmApp("web", "../web").WithReference(api).WithHttpEndpoint(env: "PORT");

builder.Build().Run();
~~~

